Facts:
Mamerto Narvaez was a tenant and occupant of a parcel of land in Maitum, South Cotabato. He had built his house and a poultry and piggery farm on the lot. The land was part of a larger tract owned or leased by the victims, Davis Fleischer and Flaviano Rubia. A boundary and tenancy dispute had arisen between Narvaez and Fleischer, resulting in pending civil litigation.

On August 22, 1968, while Narvaez was resting inside his house, Fleischer and Rubia arrived with laborers and began constructing a barbed-wire fence directly through Narvaez’s compound. The fence was built so close that it enclosed Narvaez’s house, effectively cutting off his access to the road, blocking his driveway, and depriving his family and livestock of access to the premises.

Narvaez woke up, looked out the window, and saw the victims actively erecting the fence. He pleaded with Fleischer: "Don't fence, Boss, as I cannot go out, let us talk about it." Fleischer brusquely dismissed him and replied: "No, gadget, go ahead, fence it." Narvaez made another appeal: "Boss, talk first; do not fence it." Fleischer retorted: "If you want to live, do not take the law into your own hands; wait for the decision, so go home."

Angered and feeling utterly degraded, Narvaez picked up his licensed shotgun, positioned himself at the window, and fired at Fleischer, killing him. Rubia attempted to draw a weapon from his jeep or move toward cover, and Narvaez fired at and killed him as well. Immediately after, Narvaez surrendered to the authorities.
The Court of First Instance (CFI) of South Cotabato convicted Narvaez of two counts of Murder, appreciating treachery and evident premeditation, and sentenced him to reclusion perpetua.

Issue: 
1.	Whether defense of property rights under Article 11, paragraph 1 of the Revised Penal Code constitutes legitimate defense against unlawful aggression.
2.	Whether appellant Mamerto Narvaez is entitled to the justifying circumstance of complete self-defense, or merely incomplete self-defense.
3.	Whether the killings were attended by treachery or evident premeditation.
Ruling: 

1. Yes. Unlawful aggression extends to violent, arbitrary aggression against property rights.
Under Article 11, paragraph 1 of the Revised Penal Code, a person may act in defense not only of his person or rights, but specifically of his property rights. The victims’ act of forcibly and arbitrarily fencing off appellant’s dwelling, denying him ingress and egress, and destroying his peaceful possession—without any court order, writ of execution, or legal process—constituted an unlawful, aggressive usurpation of real property rights. It was an act of taking the law into their own hands, which provided the element of unlawful aggression.

2. Appellant was entitled only to incomplete self-defense (defense of property).
While unlawful aggression was present, the Court held that the second requisite—reasonable necessity of the means employed—was absent:
•	Killing to Defend Mere Property Is Disproportionate: The law does not permit the taking of human life purely to protect property rights, except where the aggression against property also constitutes an imminent, mortal threat to life or physical integrity.
•	Because the victims, though armed and high-handed, were not directly aiming weapons at Narvaez or placing his life in immediate danger at the exact moment he fired, shooting them dead with a shotgun was excessive.
•	However, because the indispensable requisite of unlawful aggression was present, Narvaez was entitled to the privileged mitigating circumstance of incomplete self-defense under Article 13(1) in relation to Article 69 of the RPC, which warrants a reduction of penalty by one or two degrees.

3. Treachery and evident premeditation were absent; crime is Homicide, not Murder.
•	No Treachery: The shooting was preceded by an open verbal exchange and warnings from the window; the victims were fully aware of Narvaez's presence and vehement objection.
•	No Evident Premeditation: The act was not planned; it was an explosive reaction provoked by the ongoing illegal fencing of his home.

Ratio Decidendi:

Dispositive:
Question:
Why are the accused in these cases not entitled to self-defense?
Answer:
1. Lack of Reasonable Necessity of the Means Employed
While the Supreme Court recognized that the victims committed unlawful aggression against Narvaez's property rights by forcibly fencing off his house and blocking his access to the highway without a court order, shooting them dead with a shotgun was grossly disproportionate:
•	The Doctrine on Defense of Property: The taking of human life is never justified purely to protect or defend property, unless the aggression against property is coupled with an actual, imminent, and mortal threat to the defender's life or physical integrity.
•	Absence of Immediate Mortal Danger: At the exact moment Narvaez aimed and pulled the trigger, the victims (Fleischer and Rubia) were supervising laborers and constructing a fence. Although they had sidearms and used abusive, overbearing language, they were not pointing weapons at Narvaez, firing at his house, or attempting to assault him physically.
•	Excessive Force: Deploying lethal firearm fire from a second-story window to halt the construction of a barbed-wire fence exceeded the rational necessity required to repel the unlawful intrusion.
2. Consequence: Entitlement to Incomplete Self-Defense Only
Because the first and indispensable condition—unlawful aggression—was legally present (directed at his property rights), but the second requisite (reasonable necessity of the means) was absent:
•	Narvaez could not be completely exonerated under the justifying circumstance of Article 11(1).
•	Instead, the Court credited him with incomplete self-defense as a privileged mitigating circumstance under Article 13, paragraph 1 in relation to Article 69 of the RPC.
This privileged mitigation, combined with ordinary mitigating circumstances (voluntary surrender and passion/obfuscation or immediate vindication of a grave offense), lowered his penalty by two degrees, reducing his conviction from Murder to Homicide and resulting in his immediate release due to time already served.

